k at  the toolkit  it's kind of composed of two levels  there's a data service kind of a  back-end data service which  is responsible for proxying  api requests to looker and then  on the front end sdk side there is some  capabilities to  build data visualizations in a more  kind of recurring way so we'll take a  look at each of those two  in turn the architecture of  the application is composed of a series  of micro services each  really in charge of their own area of  concern so there's one that does  authentication there's another  that does logging and there is also one  of course that surfaces  thought spot-backed data to power  front-end visualizations  we elected to integrate into this  existing architecture by building a  parallel  looker-backed data service this had the  huge advantage of not really requiring  us to rip and replace  any part of the existing architecture if  we had done so it probably would have  brought  product development to a standstill and  probably would have been met with a lot  of  resistance let's say resistance to  change so instead we just built this  parallel service  and without a lot of fuss we just  started doing it a better  and faster way and let the results speak  for themselves  in a pretty short amount of time  engineering teams fairly organically  just started moving  over to using the looker data back  service  because it just made their jobs easier  and allowed them to do a lot more with a  lot less  the other really good advantage about  this kind of data as a sidecar service  is that it exists somewhat decoupled  from the application context in which  it was developed which means it can be  reused in a whole variety of different  ways  we've essentially built a capability as  much as we've built a specific solution  and the capability can be used to for  example power other internal  applications or  even externally facing applications that  our customer might want to monetize  given the constraints of this particular  enterprise environment  our data service wasn't built in the  cloud but  it's important to note that in other  circumstances you could use  cloud-based api type services like  apigee for example  to play the same role in this kind of  architecture  okay so with that discussion of the data  service let's pivot a little bit  and talk about um the development  toolkit  that we built that handles the front end  visualizations  for the front end sdk we built a series  of reusable react components  each one representing a different data  visualization type  we developed the interface you're  looking at here which exposes each of  the different vis components  and acts kind of like live documentation  allowing developers  and product owners and ux designers to  collaborate on the best way  to tell the given data story anyone with  access can adjust the vis configuration  parameters  to get the vis just right prior to  committing any code  we then connected these components to  the look or data service we just spoke  about  and parameterized them such that we can  pass the id  of any pre-built looker query object  connect it to the viz and essentially  render the results here in real time  once everybody's happy with the results  it's just a matter really of copying a  couple of lines of generated code  into the react application code base  so by way of both the looker data  service we spoke about earlier  and this front-end sdk we've been able  to  leverage existing capabilities within  looker  and build a scalable architecture that  vastly accelerates time to value for our  customers  while also decreasing complexity the net  result on this engagement  has been a 10x increase in the speed it  takes to publish new insights in the  application  and all of that with about a third of  the engineering resources required  so now with relevant and timely insights  at their disposal  these retail merchants are using the  application in much higher numbers  and with much more regularity than ever  before  so from a purely quantitative  perspective  i think it's fair to say achievement  unlocked but  honestly the qualitative outcomes of  this engagement to me are  even more exciting what we delivered  initially was a series of capabilities  offering speed and flexibility for the  product team and the engineering team  but subsequently we've taken these same  capabilities and  offered them to end users allowing them  really for the first time to  self-service insights  and as a result the product has moved  from this very  top-down one-size-fits-all approach to  analytics  to one that really promotes much greater  data democratization and ultimately  better decision making throughout the  business the net result is an  organization  much better prepared to steer the course  through challenging economic  environments so that pretty much wraps  up what i want to share about this  engagement  i do want to encourage you to explore  how looker's platform capabilities  can transform your own data culture and  in turn your business  with that i'm going to go ahead and hand  it back to mike  all right thank you nick for that uh  walkthrough of the case study  so i really hope this talk gives you  guys a good sense of  the looker platform's capabilities how  it works and what it can offer you  hopefully you're imagining how it can  enable you and your team to build some  really cool new data experiences  developing with the right tools can  really decrease the complexity  and also maintenance costs of our rich  applications  and this helps us rapidly build more and  more powerful applications as well  it's important really to note that  looker is really just  a piece of the tool set within google's  development  environment in google's ecosystem  there are lots of other tools designed  with developers and their applications  in mind  together with by using all of these  tools correctly you can really  accelerate development  and enable your ability 